{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And may He send you your other brother and Benjamin</b> — It appears according to the plain sense that Simeon was not favored by his father because of the matter of Shechem, and therefore he did not say \"Simeon my son and Benjamin,\" for he would not mention him by name, just as he had left him in Egypt for many days, and had there been bread in his house he would not yet have sent Benjamin, and would have left him [Simeon] in Egypt. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 43:14), \"The holy spirit sparkled in him, to include Joseph.\" And in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 92:3) they also said: \"And may He send you your brother\" — this is Joseph; \"another\" — this is Simeon. And this is correct, for he set his mind at the time of prayer to pray in general terms also about the other, perhaps he is still alive. And another interpretation they gave there in Bereshit Rabbah: they said, Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi interpreted the verse with reference to the exiles: \"And El Shaddai give you mercy before the man\" — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Exodus 15:3), \"YHWH is a man of war,\" and it is written (Psalms 106:46), \"And He gave them mercy\"; \"and may He send you your brother\" — these are the tribes; \"another and Benjamin\" — this is Judah and Benjamin; \"and I, as I have been bereaved\" — in the first destruction, \"I am bereaved\" — in the second destruction, I will not be bereaved any more — this is their language, of blessed memory. And the intention is that Jacob's descent to Egypt alludes to our exile at the hand of Edom, as I shall explain (beginning of Parashat Vayehi), and the prophet perceived the matter from its beginning and prayed in general terms for his hour and for the generations. And this verse according to their midrash has a great secret, for he said \"And El Shaddai\" — in the attribute of judgment — \"will give you mercy\" before Him: He will raise you from the attribute of judgment to the attribute of mercy. And the enlightened will understand:" "<b>And I, as I have been bereaved, I am bereaved</b> — And I, until your return, will be bereaved through uncertainty: as I have been bereaved of Joseph and Simeon, I am bereaved of Benjamin — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 43:14). But the correct interpretation is that he says: you will not be able to add bereavement upon me, for I am already bereaved; he will be consoled for all that comes upon him by virtue of the excessiveness of his grief over Joseph. And similarly, \"and as I have perished, I have perished\" (Esther 4:16), meaning, I am already lost, and if He puts me to death, it will not add destruction upon me."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {0 "The word אחיכם (your brother) in the midrashic reading is rendered by Ramban in connection with the cited proof texts; the syntax of the kabbalistic passage at the end is rendered hyper-literally, as it is textually dense and possibly elliptical."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:25.436059547Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}